idge right here which sort  of marks the center of the North  Atlantic Basin and what we found was  pretty much uniformly high plastic it  doesn't ever decrease we in other words  we didn't find the edge of the  accumulation zone so here kind of a  similar figure different color scheme  but plastic concentration where the  highest values are purple and then this  green and pretty much uniformly more  than 10,000 pieces per kilometer squared  whereas in the rest of the basin just  quickly going back to this picture I  forgot to mention the Caribbean by the  way relatively low plastic concentration  similarly up here even where those high  population centers relatively low  plastic concentrations so and you know  in this picture there's really almost no  blue we're finding very high plastic  concentrations extending the length of  the cruise track and in fact we had the  record breaker more than 23,000 pieces  of plastic in a single net tow so those  people were picking plastic for two days  and in fact at some point cut it off and  just had to estimate how much was in the  net so there's no indication that that  is decreasing unfortunately it didn't  answer our question we need to go out  there again and keep going  across the basin if you're interested in  learning about the expedition we do it  did have a website that discuss daily  science results there are photos and  videos from see it was an outreach tool  so if you're interested I encourage you  to visit that website okay so what do we  know about other oceans just briefly  what I'm going to show you is a video of  some numerical model results this is a  numerical model that basically was  constructed to have artificial plastic  pieces seeded uniformly across the world  ocean and then the ocean was turned on  and the surface karen's currents were  allowed to carry it where they may and  that evolution was observed for ten  years and so the idea was to try to  understand where globally these  accumulation zones might be so here we  have this image and I just think it's  pretty powerful image you notice it  started out all blue so that was the  uniformly high concentration and over  time you see that this artificial debris  moves away from the equator that's  because the currents are diverging  they're carrying carrying material away  sorry this repeats so you see this this  diversion so growing where there's no  debris and then you can see in these  subtropical gyre regions the North  Atlantic like we've talked about but  also the North Pacific in the so-called  North Pacific Garbage Patch there are  also these accumulation zones and the  other subtropical gyre Xin the southern  basins and also in the Indian Ocean so  right now there are some expeditions  that are going out for the first time to  try to measure these areas to see if  there is in fact floating plastic debris  there and and so far all indications are  yes but we don't know you know again  this is this this model is assuming a  uniformly high concentration at the  start obviously the sources of plastic  to the ocean are varied I mean we can't  assume that this was the initial  condition for our real ocean so while  these the mechanisms exist we really  have no idea how much debris we will  find in any of these places ok I'm going  to shift gears a little bit and start  talking you talk quickly about the  trends in plastic concentration that we  have observed over the  time period of our data sets so what I'm  showing you here is annually average  plastic concentration in that  accumulation zone so in that 20 22 38  degrees north latitude range as a  function of time over the course of our  data set the 22 year time period and the  black lines again are the standard error  associated with that and first thing you  notice is well gee there's a lot of up  and down there's just a lot of  variability and in fact that has been a  little bit of a beast we have worked  long and hard to try to get a decent  picture from that because it is highly  variable and if you look even on the if  you look you know net total net tose  that stations that are about 30 or 50  miles apart you can get a huge whopping  amount of plastic in one and very little  in the next so there's just a lot of  spatial variability and this is telling  us there's also a lot of temporal  variability in the record and in fact if  you fit a trend line it's flat with an  r-squared of 0 so we are observing no  trend in plastic accumulation in the  Atlantic Ocean and this was surprising  to us given the drastic increase in  global production of plastic over this  time period so the question really is  all right why do we not see a trend so  we have to think about the sources of  plastic to the ocean we also have to  think about how plastic is being removed  from the surface of the ocean and again  to remind you we're talking about  plastic larger than a third of a  millimeter in size floating at the sea  surface this is not a picture of exact  of entirely what is in the ocean but in  that particular data set so what is  acting to potentially remove plastic  from the surface of the ocean so first  let's talk about the source we thought  well you know we we need some kind of  number about how much plastic might be  entering the ocean and because the  sources are so dispersed widespread and  not well quantified we couldn't we  couldn't estimate it directly so instead  we had to look at proxy record which is  provided by the Environmental Protection  Agency they have reports of you know  municipal solid waste in the United  States over back since nineteen sixty so  what I'm showing here is a time series  of plastic waste generation how much  waste is generated and then how much  waste is discarded and the difference  between these is the amount that was  actually recycled  recycling is being taken into account  into this curve so plastic waste  discards were greatly increasing over  this time period so given this we feel  like it is a reasonable assumption to  say that the amount of plastic entering  the ocean over that time period  increased it is important to note also  this curve doesn't take into a cab  dumping from ships it also doesn't take  into account anything about those resin  pellets nonetheless we are assuming it's  increasing so we have to investigate  other aspects of the trend first could  there be a sampling bias or variability  to surface ocean currents these are  processed questions did we does our  figure actually represent what we think  it does and we we think it does so I'm  not going to go into those details we  thought about the source a little more  carefully thought well maybe even though  more plastic as being being and entered  into the waste stream maybe it's not the  kind that floats and actually the EPA  does break this down by material and so  we looked at the curve of buoyant resins  the floating plastics and in fact those  increased by about twenty-five percent  over the time period so again we're  pretty convinced the amount of plastic  that floats entering the ocean has  increased so then we had to look at  marine removal Beckett mechanisms what  might be taking it away we know that  plastic pieces probably get to be  smaller than a third of a millimeter and  we're not measuring them but we don't  know how small I get or how or how much  is out there sedimentation we know that  there can be those biofilms bear on the  plastics other interesting organisms  grow on the plastic could make them  dense enough that they have to sink away  from the ocean they sink away from the  ocean surface again to where we can't  measure them no estimate no good  quantification of that short deposition  we know that plastic is washing up on  shore as well as being removed from  shore and we do have records from East  Coast beaches which show that it's  highly variable seasonally dependent  depends on the beach so this doesn't  answer our question and then finally we  know that organisms of various at  various parts of the trophic level are  eating plastic but we don't know who  eats it we don't know what happens after  it's eaten we don't know what happens  after the organism dies so really these  are these are things that can remove it  but we have no idea how the  any of those terms are very briefly we  did find one really important trend in  our data set and that's in those resin  pellets so we did distinguish those when  picking them out the students pellets  versus pieces and over the entire region  we did see a statistically significant  decrease in pellet concentration over  time and we believe this is in direct  response to plastic industries response  to an EPA study implementing measures to  reduce the the spillage of pellet  transport and handling the spillage and  the transport and handling processes so  we think that this actually is a really  nice demonstration of changing changing  habits onshore that are then being  observed in the ocean and could probably  be a model study ok wrapping up quickly  because we're getting in here and  there's a class outstanding questions  pretty much all of them are still  outstanding how much plastic is in the  ocean where is it located we can say  generally we know where to look for it  now but we again because of the spatial  variability we haven't looked in  vertically in the water column this is  still very much an open question  although the question is a bit more  refined what is the fate of plastic  marine debris I just listed a number of  mechanisms that may be taking it away  from the surface but we don't know what  happens to it there or in other parts of  the ocean and what are the biological  and chemical implications we still just  don't know so in summary essays plastic  marine debris dataset is the longest and  most extensive record of plastic marine  debris in any ocean and I think these  data provide a really important baseline  again undergraduates mostly responsible  for this hope I've convinced you the  plastic debris does accumulate in the  subtropical convergence zone of the  Western North Atlantic and likely and  other ocean basins although please don't  call it a garbage patch and then despite  a likely increase in source input we do  not observe a trend in the amount of  debris in the North Atlantic and we  don't know why yet we don't know where  it's going this is the case of the  missing debris and then finally just to  wrap it up our garbage patch is a  problem you might think let's just clean  it up what's the big deal I think you  know after looking at what these pieces  look like it's just not feasible and we  can talk about why you might also think  well maybe these new biopolymers will  prevent the problem from getting worse  and just a very strong word of warning  about when you see the labeled  biodegradable on  kind of product you don't know what that  means some of these products are in fact  biodegradable but only in very specific  environmental facilities we have no idea  what happens to the marine environment  so in my opinion the real solution is  controlling the source of debris and  that means all of you perhaps reducing  the amount of packaging that you consume  and also from the manufacturers side of  things but also then reusing and  recycling what you do use and I guess  I'll leave it there just to acknowledge  acknowledgments of my co-authors and  also the the work of the students that  really do the bulk of the work and if  anybody is interested there are reprints  of the article outside and also  information about SCA and I'm also happy  to you know chat with you afterwards  thanks very much
 

